course: chinese-street
lesson: 18
title: The body
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: At Lín's house you trip over the cat and his mum rushes in: "nǎr téng?" — "where does it hurt?" — and you point and say "zhèr téng", it hurts here.
explain: Body words are short and easy to hear: tóu (toe, rising) is head, shǒu (show, dipping) is hand, jiǎo (jyow) is foot, dùzi (DOO-dz) is tummy, so "dùzi téng" is a tummy ache.
explain: Tones turn body parts into numbers and food: ěr (dipping) is in ěrduo, ears, but èr (falling) is two; jiǎo is a foot, but jiǎozi are dumplings and jiào is "to be called".
explain: Some body words make every playground giggle: pìgu (PEE-goo) is bottom, a childish word, not a rude one; but one word for mouth turns up in a rude "shut up!", so say "bié shuō le" instead.
explain: Never mock the shape of someone's eyes or pull your eyes to "look Chinese": that is racism, not a joke.
fact: "tóu" (rising) means head; "tóu téng" is a headache, and grannies may fondly touch a child's head.
fact: "yǎnjing" means eyes; "bì shàng yǎnjing" means close your eyes; mocking eye shapes or pulling your eyes is racism.
fact: "ěrduo" means ears; "nǐ de ěrduo hóng le" (your ears have gone red) means you're embarrassed; ěr dips, while èr (two) falls.
fact: "bízi" means nose; "liú bítì" is a runny nose, and "wā bíshǐ" (picking your nose) is a word kids find hilarious.
fact: "zuǐba" means mouth; the dentist says "zhāng zuǐ", open your mouth; zuǐ also turns up in a rude "shut up!", and the soft way is "bié shuō le".
fact: "yá" (rising) means tooth or teeth; "wǒ diào yá le!" is "my tooth fell out!"; in some Chinese families a lost bottom tooth is thrown onto the roof, not left for a fairy.
fact: "shǒu" (dipping) means hand; "xǐ shǒu" means wash your hands, before eating; shòu (falling) means thin.
fact: "jiǎo" (dipping) means foot; "wǒ de jiǎo téng" is "my foot hurts"; jiǎozi are dumplings and jiào is "to be called".
fact: "dùzi" means tummy; "dùzi téng" is tummy ache, and "dùzi jiào le" (my tummy is calling) means it rumbled.
fact: "tuǐ" means leg; "wǒ de tuǐ suān le" is "my legs are aching"; jītuǐ (chicken leg) is a favourite food.
fact: "pìgu" means bottom or bum; it is a childish word, not a rude one, and "fàng pì" (to fart) makes every playground laugh.
fact: "téng" (rising) means "it hurts"; "nǎr téng?" is "where does it hurt?", "zhèr téng" is "it hurts here", and "bù téng" is "it doesn't hurt".
exercise: You bang your head on the bunk bed. What hurts? | tóu | jiǎo | tuǐ | shǒu
exercise: Lín's mum says "bì shàng yǎnjing" before a surprise. What do you do? | Close your eyes | Open your mouth wide | Wash your hands first | Hold your nose tight
exercise: Lín's granny laughs: "nǐ de ěrduo hóng le!". What does she mean? | You're embarrassed, your ears went red | You've got an ear infection | You've grown two ears taller | Your ears are cold
exercise: Lín has a cold and needs a tissue. What's running? | bízi, his nose | tuǐ, his leg | yá, his tooth | shǒu, his hand
exercise: Lín's little brother is "wā bíshǐ" at the table. What is he doing? | Picking his nose | Blowing his nose | Holding his breath | Pulling a face
exercise: At the dentist you hear "zhāng zuǐ". What do you do? | Open your mouth | Close your eyes tight | Hold out your hand | Lift up your foot
exercise: Lín shows a gap in his smile: "wǒ diào yá le!". What happened? | His tooth fell out | He bit his tongue | He lost his hat | He hurt his foot
exercise: Lín's mum calls "xǐ shǒu!" before dinner. What must you do? | Wash your hands | Wash your feet first | Brush your teeth | Sit down and close your eyes
exercise: You stub your toe. How do you say "my foot hurts"? | wǒ de jiǎo téng | wǒ de jiǎozi téng | wǒ de tóu téng | wǒ de yá téng
exercise: Lín groans "dùzi téng" after too many sweets. What's wrong? | Tummy ache | Headache | Sore leg | Earache
exercise: Lín's tummy rumbles in class and he whispers "dùzi jiào le". What does he mean? | My tummy is rumbling | My tummy hurts badly | My tummy is full | My tummy is ticklish
exercise: After a long walk Lín moans "wǒ de tuǐ suān le". What aches? | His legs | His teeth | His sore ears | His tired eyes
exercise: Lín's sister falls on her bottom and giggles "pìgu!". Is pìgu a swear word? | No, it's a childish word for bottom | Yes, never say it, not even at home | Yes, it's very rude to all adults | No, it means "oops"
exercise: You hurt yourself in the playground and Lín's mum asks "nǎr téng?". What is she asking? | Where does it hurt? | Why did you fall? | Who pushed you? | Do you want water?
exercise: How do you say "it doesn't hurt"? | bù téng | nǎr téng | hǎo téng | zhèr téng
exercise: Lín's friend pulls his eyes sideways to "look Chinese". What is that? | Racism, not a joke | A harmless joke | A Chinese greeting | A game everyone plays
exercise: A friend is arguing loudly and you want him to stop, softly. What do you say? | bié shuō le | zhāng zuǐ | bì shàng yǎnjing | xǐ shǒu
test: my cousin says jītuǐ is a body part, chicken's leg so it's your leg. right? | No, jītuǐ is chicken leg, a food | Yes, jītuǐ is your own leg, innit | Yes, it means a sore leg after running | No, it means a chicken's foot
test: You want to say "I've got two ears" but your voice falls on the first bit: èrduo. What sounds wrong? | èr falling is "two", not "ear" | Nothing, both tones are fine for ears | èr falling means "nose" | èr falling is a rude word for ears
test: You ask for jiǎozi when you meant your foot hurts. What did Lín's mum hear? | Something about dumplings | Something about your head | Something about your name | Something about your teeth
test: Lín says "wǒ hěn shòu" with a falling tone, not "shǒu". What did he say? | I'm thin, not anything about hands | My hand is big | I wash my hands | My hand hurts
test: you lost a tooth at Lín's house and his granny wants to throw it on the roof. weird or what? | It's a real custom in some Chinese families | It's a punishment for losing it at her house | It means she's cross with you | It's a joke, nobody really does that
test: In a cartoon a boy says "zhèr téng" and points to his tummy. What is he saying? | It hurts here | It's fine here | Tickle me here | I'm full up here
test: Lín's mate shouts something rude with "zuǐ" in it at a dinner lady. What did it probably mean? | A rude "shut up!" | "Open your mouth, please" | "Your food is tasty" | "I'm hungry"
test: Everyone in the playground laughs when someone says "fàng pì". Why? | It means to fart | It means a swear | It means a bum smack | It means to cry
test: Which is the only body part here with a rising tone, like a surprised "huh?"? | tóu, head | shǒu, hand | jiǎo, foot | tuǐ, leg
test: What would happen if you told Lín's mum "bù téng" after a fall? | She'd know it doesn't hurt | She'd think it hurts a lot | She'd think you're hungry | She'd call a doctor at once
test: Lín's ears turn red when the teacher reads his poem aloud. What does his granny say? | nǐ de ěrduo hóng le | nǐ de bízi hóng le | nǐ de pìgu téng le | nǐ de tóu téng le
exam: Identify the expression whereby an adult customarily enquires as to the location of a child's pain. | nǎr téng? | zhèr téng | bù téng | dùzi jiào le
exam: It is given that the syllables ěr and èr differ solely in tone. Select the statement that accurately characterises their respective meanings. | ěr (dipping) pertains to the ear; èr (falling) denotes two | ěr denotes two; èr pertains to the ear and to hearing | Both denote the ear | Both denote the number two, whatever the tone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the word "pìgu"? | It is a childish term for the bottom, not considered obscene | It is a grave obscenity, never to be uttered by children | It denotes the stomach | It is a formal medical term for the leg
exam: Determine the classification appropriate to the act of pulling one's eyes so as to imitate persons of Chinese descent. | Racist mockery, not a joke | A traditional greeting | A playground game without consequence | A medical examination
